</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Comparison Experiments</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conducted experiments to evaluate our method’s grounding performance and consistency, demonstrating the effectiveness of our method, TCAS, in improving temporal logic consistency and understanding.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.1 </span>Baselines</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our method’s effectiveness by comparing it with several baseline models and approaches. For open-source models, direct comparison without fine-tuning would be unfair, as no existing modeling methods specifically target consistency enhancement. Therefore, we compare our approach with standard fine-tuning methods, specifically <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S</span>upervised <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F</span>ine-<span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">T</span>uning (SFT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2024)</a></cite>, applied to several selected backbones. The results of other models without tuning include GPT-4o <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI (2024)</a></cite>, Gemini-1.5 Flash <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team (2024)</a></cite>, and so on, listed in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS4" title="B.4 Other Baselines ‣ Appendix B More Details about Experiments ‣ Improving Temporal Understanding Logic Consistency in Video-Language Models via Attention Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Notably, some models, such as VTG-LLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al. (2025)</a></cite>, focus on improving temporal understanding without addressing consistency issues. So we only compare their grounding performance, excluding consistency metrics.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.2 </span>Main Results</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present the comparison results on consistency (Table <a href="#S3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 3 Attention Enhancement ‣ Improving Temporal Understanding Logic Consistency in Video-Language Models via Attention Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) and grounding performance (Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.1.5 Implementation Details ‣ 4.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Improving Temporal Understanding Logic Consistency in Video-Language Models via Attention Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). The best results are in bold or underlined in different areas. Light gray rows indicate results from our method, TCAS.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.3 </span>Discussion</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We focus on the following phenomena:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1). The improvements brought by TCAS are evident across different models, training datasets, and benchmarks.</span> As shown in Table <a href="#S3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 3 Attention Enhancement ‣ Improving Temporal Understanding Logic Consistency in Video-Language Models via Attention Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, TCAS achieves favorable results in all comparisons, indicating that TCAS is not dependent on specific models, benchmarks, or training annotations and can robustly enhance model performance and consistency. The performance improvement on ActivityNet-CON is relatively smaller, which is due to the noisier data distribution of this dataset, where event descriptions are presented in long sentences that deviate from the general understanding of the concept of events. Consequently, all methods and models perform worse on this dataset, making the smaller improvement acceptable.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">2). TCAS improves both grounding and consistency performance.</span> On the classic VTG benchmarks, Charades-STA and ActivityNet-Captions, TCAS achieves SOTA performance among Video-LLMs methods. This suggests that inconsistent temporal understanding is a key limitation for the grounding performance of these models. By enhancing attention discriminability, TCAS improves temporal logic consistency, thereby boosting grounding performance and advancing video temporal understanding.</p>
</div>
